# Sway(fonn) — startup layout
#
# One line per app:   <workspace number>   <command>
# Blank lines and #-comments are ignored. Everything after the first token is
# the command, run through a shell, so flags / pipes / $HOME all work.
#
# Use `-` instead of a number for an app that should NOT own a screen. Those are
# started last, after the numbered screens are placed, so a window that is on
# its way to the scratchpad can't be mistaken for a screen's app arriving.
#
# Screens open in order. sway-layout switches to the workspace BEFORE launching
# and waits for the window to appear before moving on — that is what puts each
# app on the right screen without identifying windows by app_id or class, which
# is brittle here: Slack and Obsidian are XWayland, Obsidian and Slack are
# flatpaks, and ghostty refuses to start if --class isn't reverse-DNS.
#
# A workspace that already has a window on it is left alone, so re-running this
# mid-session won't open a second copy of anything.

1   ghostty
# zen-space sets Zen's `zen.workspaces.active` pref to the named Space's UUID
# just before launching, because Zen has no command-line flag for Spaces. The
# Space is looked up by name, so recreating it in Zen won't break this line.
2   zen-space CareerPlug

# The `slack` on PATH is the snap (/snap/bin/slack). It starts a process but
# never maps a window on this machine — that is why `exec slack` in the old
# config never actually gave you Slack. The flatpak build works, so use it.
3   flatpak run com.slack.Slack

4   flatpak run md.obsidian.Obsidian

# Scratchpad apps — no screen of their own. Mod+minus summons the last one.
-   thunderbird
-   ticktick
